+    // worker. They are gone with the design that needed them: a worker is now a
+    // whole instantiation, so its globals start at the module's initial values
+    // and the guest's own thread-start code installs per-thread state from its
+    // context argument. That is not merely simpler, it is the correct
+    // semantics — under shared-everything-threads an unshared global is
+    // per-THREAD state (canonically `__stack_pointer`), and inheriting the
+    // spawner's value put two threads on one C shadow stack.
+
+    /// Re-point the defined memory `index` of this instance at `shared`,
+    /// which must be another instantiation's `SharedMemory` of a compatible
